       "text": "Cycling outdoors in Kuching with the haze requires caution. Current air quality readings for Kuching place the Air Quality Index (AQI) around the **Moderate** range (roughly 70–75). \n\nWhile a \"Moderate\" rating means the air is generally acceptable for most people, **strenuous outdoor exercise like cycling changes the equation**:\n* **Increased Breathing Rate:** When you cycle, you breathe deeper and faster, pulling significantly more fine particulate matter ($\\text{PM}_{2.5}$) deep into your lungs than you would resting or walking.\n* **Personal Sensitivity:** If you have pre-existing respiratory conditions (like asthma), cardiovascular issues, or if you are sensitive to smoke and haze, even moderate air quality can trigger throat irritation, coughing, or shortness of breath.\n\n### Recommendations:\n1. **Check the Latest Local Updates:** Look up the latest reading on the Malaysian Department of Environment's APIMS system or local apps right before you head out, as haze conditions can shift quickly.\n2. **Listen to Your Body:** If you choose to ride, keep the intensity light-to-moderate, shorten your route, and stop immediately if you feel any chest tightness, throat irritation, or dizziness.\n3. **Consider Indoor Alternatives:** If you are part of a sensitive group, or if the haze visibility thickens into the evening, it is safer to postpone your ride or move your workout indoors.",
